  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 72.42529296875pt)[#text(size: 13.0pt)[The Pirate Stronghold and Ming Restoration ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 93.55126953125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[During the Hongwu era, an armed leader named Chen Zuyi fled from Guangdong with his family ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 108.09716796875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[and followers, seizing dominance over the Palembang haven. Operating with naval impunity, ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 122.64306640625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Chen plundered passing commercial fleets and imposed tyrannical levies on travelers. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 149.18994140625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[When Zheng Ho’s grand armada arrived during the first voyage (1405–1407), another prominent ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 163.73583984375pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Chinese resident, Shih Chin-ch'ing, reported the pirate confederacy's depredations to the ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 178.28173828125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[imperial envoys. In the ensuing confrontation, Ming marines and war junks engaged Chen Zuyi’s ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 192.82861328125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[fleet, killing thousands of brigands, capturing several pirate vessels, and taking Chen captive. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 207.37451171875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Escorted back to China, Chen was publicly executed at Nanjing. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 233.92138671875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[In recognition of his loyalty, the imperial court invested Shih Chin-ch'ing with a ceremonial hat, ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 248.46728515625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[sash, and official credentials, confirming him as principal pacification chief over Old Haven. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 263.01416015625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Following Shih Chin-ch'ing's death, governance passed to his capable daughter, Shih Erh-chieh, ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 277.55908203125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[who exercised full executive authority over appointments, rewards, and legal punishments while ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 292.10595703125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[maintaining regular tributary embassies to China. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 318.71728515625pt)[#text(size: 13.0pt)[Commodities, Fauna, and Market Trade ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 72.0pt, dy: 339.84326171875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Old Haven functioned as an active trading post for rare forest and maritime commodities: ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 90.0pt, dy: 366.39013671875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[●​]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 99.69732666015625pt, dy: 366.39013671875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 366.39013671875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Aromatics and Resins:]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 227.11602783203125pt, dy: 366.39013671875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ The country yielded high-grade laka-wood, sinking eaglewood, ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 380.93603515625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[and fragrant benzoin (]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 215.54953002929688pt, dy: 380.93603515625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[chin-yin]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 253.42642211914062pt, dy: 380.93603515625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ incense), an aromatic resin prized for silver inlaying and ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 395.48291015625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[sacred offerings. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 90.0pt, dy: 410.02783203125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[●​]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 99.69732666015625pt, dy: 410.02783203125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 410.02783203125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Avian Rarities:]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 184.56394958496094pt, dy: 410.02783203125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ The forests produced the helmeted hornbill (the "crane's crest bird"), ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 424.57470703125pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[whose thick, carmine-colored cranial bone was carved into archers' rings, snuff boxes, ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 439.12060546875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[and dagger hilts; travelers also encountered the flightless cassowary ("fire fowl"), ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 453.66748046875pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[renowned for its sharp plumage and defensive claws. ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 90.0pt, dy: 468.21337890625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[●​]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 99.69732666015625pt, dy: 468.21337890625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ ]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 108.0pt, dy: 468.21337890625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[Fauna:]]
  #place(top + left, dx: 144.03054809570312pt, dy: 468.21337890625pt)[#text(size: 11.0pt)[ Upland tracts sheltered the Malayan tapir, described by Ma Huan as an unusual ]]
